cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this work, please cite it as below — and please also credit the underlying data,
  the Malaysian Election Corpus (MECo) by Thevesh Thevananthan.
title: "Nadi Demokrasi: Malaysia's Democracy in Numbers"
abstract: >-
  A reproducible, citable single-page dashboard measuring seven decades of Malaysian federal general
  elections (1955–2022) with fourteen standard political-science indicators: vote-to-seat 
  disproportionality (Gallagher), the winner's seat bonus, malapportionment (Samuels–Snyder), map 
  bias and district compactness (Polsby–Popper) as measures of the gerrymandering question, the 
  winning bloc's vote share, the effective number of parties (Laakso–Taagepera), multi-cornered 
  contests, electoral volatility (Pedersen), seat turnover, marginal seats, turnout, and the 
  descriptive representation of women and ethnic communities. Read together, the indicators tell a 
  coherent story: first-past-the-post long turned modest vote leads into commanding majorities (a 
  seat bonus once worth +27 points, disproportionality above 20), but that mechanical reward has 
  since collapsed to about zero — even as unequal district boundaries (malapportionment at a record 
  ~18%) now tilt against whoever wins the popular vote, and the party system has fragmented from a 
  one-and-a-half-party dominance to a record ~3.6 effective parties. All analysis performed using 
  the Malaysian Election Corpus (MECo).
type: software
